<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Blindness of both eyes since early childhood, with no light perception, in a 65-year-old man new to the practice. The cause was never diagnosed; glaucoma is often suggested. Please examine the eyes for comfort and complications and advise whether eye pressure needs checking or treatment, and what eye care he needs from here. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within four weeks, on the same day as his four-week primary care check. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is there any active eye disease, such as inflammation, exposure, or raised pressure causing pain, that needs treatment now?</item><item>In eyes with no light perception and no pain, should eye pressure be measured or lowered?</item><item>What eye surveillance does he need, and how often?</item><item>Which symptoms should bring him back urgently?</item><item>Would any vision rehabilitation services help him now?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Charles, a 65-year-old man, is seen at the request of Dr. Fenwick. He lost his sight gradually from about age five and was blind by age seven; he says no cause was ever diagnosed. He has no eye pain, redness, discharge, light sensitivity, or headache around the eyes, and he uses no eye drops. He wears dark glasses by habit. He is independent in all daily activities, travels for work with a sighted guide in unfamiliar places, and reads with a screen reader. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Ray Charles Robinson was born in Albany, Georgia, on September 23, 1930; his mother moved him as an infant to Greenville, a small town in north Florida. [D]</item><item>Charles began to lose his sight at about age five and was blind by age seven; after he was declared legally blind, he was enrolled at the Florida School for the Deaf and Blind in St. Augustine, where he remained for nine years. [D]</item><item>The cause of his blindness is uncertain. It is described as probably congenital juvenile glaucoma, and glaucoma is often mentioned as the cause, but Charles said that nothing was ever diagnosed. [R]</item><item>In 1964 Charles was arrested for possession of heroin, and he later admitted that he had been addicted since the late 1940s. After a period of rehabilitation at a California sanitarium he stopped using drugs and received a five-year suspended sentence. [D]</item><item>Charles struggled with heroin addiction for nearly 20 years and quit &quot;cold turkey&quot; in 1965, after an arrest at the Boston airport. He later became reluctant to discuss his drug use, fearing it would taint how people viewed his work. [D]</item><item>In the year Charles began to lose his sight, his younger brother drowned. His mother died in 1945. [D]</item></list><paragraph>1 later historical claim is omitted because it describes events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

